{"meta":{"chapter_title":"AI in Education","title":"What AI Changes in Schools","description":"CAT 531 textbook section connecting what ai changes in schools to Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","course":"cat531-supplement","chapter":"03","section":"01","learning_objectives":["Analyze how the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible."],"concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_ai_in_education","cat531_supplement_what_ai_changes_in_schools","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"prereq_section_ids":["cat531-supplement/02/08"],"practice_ids":["cat531_supplement_3_1_what_ai_changes_in_schools_artifact_signal","cat531_supplement_3_1_what_ai_changes_in_schools_annotation_signal","cat531_supplement_3_1_what_ai_changes_in_schools_ai_boundary","cat531_supplement_3_1_what_ai_changes_in_schools_calibration"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":1,"top_tier_hardened":false},"content":"# What AI Changes in Schools\n\n![What AI Changes in Schools textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/03-01-what-ai-changes-in-schools.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nSchools have always absorbed new technology, from the overhead projector to the learning-management system, but generative AI is different in one specific way: it does not just deliver content or manage logistics, it produces the kind of work students are supposed to produce. When a tool can write the essay, solve the problem set, and grade the result, the boundaries that organized a classroom for a century start to move. The teacher who used to ask \"does this tool help me deliver instruction\" now has to ask a harder question: \"what part of learning is mine to protect, and what part can a machine legitimately take over?\"\n\nThis section is the on-ramp to that question for the whole module. It is not about whether AI is good or bad in the abstract. It is about learning to see, in a concrete classroom, exactly what shifts when an AI tool enters: who does the cognitive work, what gets measured, what gets cheaper, and what quietly disappears. Naming those shifts precisely is the first professional skill, because you cannot make a defensible decision about a change you cannot describe.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA middle-school principal announces that every classroom will pilot an AI \"co-teacher\" that drafts lesson plans, generates differentiated worksheets, and writes feedback on student drafts. Two teachers react differently. The first is delighted: \"This gives me back my evenings.\" The second is uneasy but cannot say why; the worksheets look fine and the feedback is grammatically clean.\n\nThe uneasy teacher is actually closer to professional judgment, because she senses that something has shifted even though the output looks polished. What she has not yet done is name the shift. Once she does, the unease becomes a decision. She notices that the AI feedback praises sentence variety and flags comma errors, but never tells a student that an argument has no evidence. The tool changed what feedback measures (surface mechanics) and quietly dropped what her unit values (reasoning). That is a nameable change, and a nameable change can be governed.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nWhat AI changes in a school is rarely the topic on the syllabus; it is the **division of cognitive labor** and the **definition of what counts as the work**. Useful analysis starts by separating three layers that people usually blur together.\n\n- **The task layer:** the visible activity (writing an essay, solving equations, planning a lesson). AI is fastest to adopt here and easiest to demonstrate.\n- **The learning layer:** the cognitive process the task was designed to develop (constructing an argument, reasoning through a procedure, anticipating student misconceptions). This is the layer schools actually exist to grow.\n- **The labor layer:** who performs the effort and who is accountable for the result.\n\nWhen AI enters, it almost always optimizes the task layer. The professional question is what happens to the learning layer underneath. A tool that completes a task while preserving the underlying cognitive work is an amplifier; a tool that completes the task by replacing the cognitive work is a substitute. The same product (an AI worksheet generator) can be either, depending on the activity it sits inside.\n\nThis is why \"is AI good for schools\" is the wrong question. The right question is a **substitution test**: for this specific use, does the tool do the task and leave the learning intact, or does it do the task by doing the learning for the student? A defensible decision answers that test with evidence, not with enthusiasm or fear.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A teacher says an AI worksheet generator 'produces clean, well-formatted differentiated worksheets in seconds.' Which layer does that observation describe?\" explanation=\"Clean, fast worksheets are a task-layer result. They tell you nothing about whether the cognitive work the worksheet was meant to provoke (the learning layer) survived. Polish is evidence the task got done, not that learning was preserved.\" options='[{\"text\":\"The learning layer, because the worksheets are differentiated\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The task layer, because polish only shows the visible activity was completed\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"The labor layer, because the teacher saved time\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"All three layers equally\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\nA second distinction does heavy work in this module. **Efficiency is not neutral.** Every claim that AI \"saves time\" hides a question about what that time was buying. The five hours a teacher spent grading essays were partly waste (formatting, mechanics) and partly diagnosis (noticing that half the class misunderstood counterargument). Automating the whole stack saves the waste and discards the diagnosis. Naming what efficiency removes, not just what it speeds up, is the core analytic move.\n\n**Legal floor.** This division-of-labor analysis sits on top of a legal floor. FERPA governs the student-record data these tools ingest, and COPPA governs data collected from children under 13; the FTC's 2025 amended COPPA Rule (effective June 23, 2025, with compliance required by April 22, 2026) expands covered data to biometric and other identifiers and limits retention. The U.S. Department of Education issued AI guidance plus updated 2025 FERPA resources, and as of 2026 roughly twenty states reference FERPA and COPPA in their K-12 AI guidance. Name the analysis first, but know the statutory floor is always underneath it.\n\n## Worked Example\n\nConsider a concrete adoption claim and the substitution test applied to it. A district markets an AI tutoring chatbot with the line: \"Students get instant, personalized help, so teachers can focus on higher-order instruction.\"\n\nRun the three layers. Task layer: the chatbot answers homework questions in seconds. Learning layer: the homework was designed to make students struggle productively, hit a wall, and develop the persistence to work through it. If the chatbot resolves every wall instantly, the productive struggle (the actual learning target) is exactly what gets removed. Labor layer: the student now produces correct homework while doing less of the thinking the homework was for.\n\nSo the marketing claim is half true and half inverted. The chatbot genuinely amplifies one use (a stuck student who has tried and needs one targeted hint) and genuinely substitutes for another (a student who outsources the first attempt). The defensible move is not \"adopt\" or \"ban.\" It is to specify the conditions: the chatbot is configured to ask a guiding question before giving an answer, and is available only after a documented first attempt. That redesign keeps the learning layer intact while still capturing the speed. The analysis, not the slogan, produced the policy.\n\n## Worked Decision: Adopting an AI Grading Assistant\n\nFollow one teacher's reasoning as a numbered before/after judgment call. The scenario is a seventh-grade ELA teacher deciding whether to adopt an AI essay-feedback assistant.\n\n1. **Before (initial draft decision):** \"I will adopt the assistant because it grades a class set in 20 minutes instead of 5 hours. Faster feedback is better feedback.\" The only evidence here is time saved.\n2. **Constraint named:** Annotate the hidden cost. The assistant scores on surface fluency and length; it cannot tell whether a student answered the prompt. Two of last week's flagged \"strong\" essays were off-topic.\n3. **Displacement check:** Annotate what the tool removes. If the assistant writes all the comments, the teacher loses the read-through that tells her which misconceptions are spreading across the class.\n4. **After (revised decision):** \"I will use the assistant to flag mechanics and length, then spend the recovered time reading the four flagged drafts myself and conferencing on argument. I reject letting it assign the grade, because argument quality is the unit's learning target and the model does not measure it.\"\n5. **Evidence trail:** The revision is justified by **Selwyn's critique that edtech is not neutral but optimizes what it can measure (Selwyn, 2016)** and by a named instructional purpose (argument, not mechanics). The rejected suggestion is recorded with its reason.\n\nThe difference between step 1 and step 4 is the whole lesson of this section: a defensible decision names the purpose, the constraint, what the tool displaces, and what was rejected.\n\nNow work the same call yourself. The branching scenario below puts you in the seat of the seventh-grade ELA teacher deciding what to do with the AI grading assistant. Each choice leads somewhere different; follow the path that keeps the learning layer with the student.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Adopting the AI essay-feedback assistant\",\"start\":\"start\",\"nodes\":{\"start\":{\"prompt\":\"The assistant grades a class set in 20 minutes instead of 5 hours. Your unit learning target is argument quality. What is your first move?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Adopt it fully; faster feedback is better feedback\",\"next\":\"full\",\"feedback\":\"You have priced only the time saved, not what the speed removes.\"},{\"label\":\"Name the learning layer before deciding anything\",\"next\":\"layer\",\"feedback\":\"Right. You cannot judge the tool until you state what cognitive work the task develops.\"},{\"label\":\"Ban it; AI grading is never trustworthy\",\"next\":\"ban\",\"feedback\":\"A blanket verdict skips the analysis; the tool amplifies some uses.\"}]},\"full\":{\"prompt\":\"Two weeks in, you have lost the read-through that told you which misconceptions were spreading. What now?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Reconfigure: let it flag mechanics, you read flagged drafts\",\"next\":\"win\",\"feedback\":\"You recovered the diagnostic information by setting a condition, not a verdict.\"},{\"label\":\"Keep going; the grades look fine\",\"next\":\"lose\",\"feedback\":\"Polished output hid that the learning-layer diagnosis disappeared.\"}]},\"ban\":{\"prompt\":\"A colleague notes the assistant reliably catches comma errors and frees time. Reconsider?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Set a condition: use it for mechanics only\",\"next\":\"win\",\"feedback\":\"You captured the amplifier use and fenced off the substitute use.\"}]},\"layer\":{\"prompt\":\"The target is argument quality, which the model cannot measure. How do you configure the tool?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Let it assign the grade to save the most time\",\"next\":\"lose\",\"feedback\":\"It would do the reasoning the assignment exists to develop.\"},{\"label\":\"Use it for mechanics and length; you conference on argument\",\"next\":\"win\",\"feedback\":\"The learning layer stays with the student while you still capture the speed.\"}]},\"win\":{\"prompt\":\"Defensible decision reached: the tool flags mechanics, you keep the argument read-through, and the rejected feature (auto-grading) is recorded with its reason.\",\"choices\":[]},\"lose\":{\"prompt\":\"Non-defensible outcome: the efficiency removed the diagnosis you teach from, and the model graded a target it cannot measure.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nWhen you face an AI tool in a classroom, run the substitution test before the convenience argument. Concretely:\n\n1. **Name the learning layer.** What cognitive work was this task designed to develop? If you cannot state it, you cannot judge the tool.\n2. **Trace the effort.** With the tool in place, who does that cognitive work, the student or the model?\n3. **Price the efficiency.** What does the time saved actually remove, and is any of it diagnostic information you rely on?\n4. **Set the condition, not the verdict.** Decide the specific configuration or use rule that keeps the learning layer intact, rather than a blanket \"yes\" or \"no.\"\n\nA tool earns adoption when it speeds the task while leaving the learning layer to the student, and when the efficiency it buys does not cost you the information you need to teach.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe most common error is treating adoption as binary: ban it or embrace it. Almost every AI tool is an amplifier for some uses and a substitute for others, so the real unit of decision is the use, not the tool. A second error is reading \"saves time\" as automatically good; time saved is only good if what it removes was waste rather than the diagnosis that grading and planning quietly provide. A third is mistaking polished output for preserved learning. Clean prose and tidy worksheets tell you the task layer was handled; they say nothing about whether the learning layer survived.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the framework to one realistic setting: a high-school history department adopting an AI tool that generates document-based-question (DBQ) practice with auto-scored rubrics.\n\nStart at the learning layer. The DBQ exists to teach students to read primary sources, weigh evidence, and build a historical argument; that reasoning is the target. Now trace effort with the tool in place. The tool generates the prompt and the sources (task layer, fine) and auto-scores the essays against a rubric. Here the substitution test gets sharp: auto-scoring can reliably check thesis presence and source citation, but it cannot judge whether a student's interpretation is historically sound, which is the discipline's whole point.\n\nPrice the efficiency. The auto-scorer saves grading hours, but those hours were where the department noticed that students treat every source as equally reliable, a misconception the tool will never surface. So the defensible configuration is specific: use the tool to generate practice and to pre-flag mechanical rubric items, then route the interpretation judgment back to teachers, and keep a monthly read of a sample to catch misconceptions the rubric misses. The department adopts the tool for what it amplifies and fences off what it would have substituted. That fence, written down with its reason, is the decision.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nA strong reader of this topic does not summarize \"AI is changing schools.\" They produce a layered map of a specific use: the task being automated, the learning underneath it, who now does the cognitive work, and what the claimed efficiency removes. As you read the rest of this module, keep a two-column note with the **claimed benefit** on the left and the **substituted learning or lost diagnosis** on the right. If the right column is empty, you have not finished the analysis; nearly every real AI use has something in it. Even AI textbooks and tutoring systems are subject to this same task, learning, and labor analysis: Google's Learn Your Way (2025), which adapts a chapter through LearnLM and Gemini 2.5 Pro and reported an ~11-percentage-point recall gain in a small study, still has to be checked against what cognitive work that lift preserves or displaces, not just the test-score number.\n\nA strong analysis can answer four questions about any classroom AI use:\n\n1. What is the learning layer this tool sits on top of?\n2. Does the tool amplify that learning or substitute for it, and for which students?\n3. What does the efficiency claim quietly remove?\n4. What configuration or use rule keeps the learning intact while still capturing the benefit?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nYour tension map is strong when a mentor teacher can see the substitution analysis, not just a pros-and-cons list. Name the specific use (not \"AI in general\"), state the learning layer it touches, and show where you drew the line between amplify and substitute. The weakest maps say \"AI has benefits and risks\"; the strongest say \"this tool amplifies X for these students under these conditions and would substitute for Y, so I governed it this way.\"\n\nA useful closing line for the artifact would read: \"I allowed this AI use for the drafting stage because the revision and argument work, which is the learning target, still belongs to the student; I fenced off the answer-generation feature because it would have done the reasoning the assignment exists to develop.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the same analysis to a setting where the stakes invert: a special-education classroom where an AI tool reads text aloud and predicts the next word for a student with a writing disability. Here, what looks like substitution in a general classroom (the tool producing words) may be legitimate access, because the learning layer is composing ideas, not the motor act of typing. The substitution test does not change, but its answer does: the tool now amplifies the real learning by removing a barrier that was never the target. Running the same framework and getting a different, well-reasoned verdict is the proof you understand it rather than a slogan about AI.\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"In your own words, explain why the SAME word-prediction tool counts as a substitute in a general writing class but as a legitimate amplifier in the special-education case. Reference the learning layer in your answer.\" answer=\"The learning layer differs across the two settings even though the tool is identical. In a general writing class the target is composing language, so a tool that produces the words does the learning for the student (substitution). In the special-education case the target is composing ideas, while the motor act of typing was never the learning target, so removing that barrier amplifies the real learning. The verdict flips because the substitution test is applied to what each task was actually designed to develop, not to the tool.\"></self-explain>\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat531_supplement_ai_in_education\",\"cat531_supplement_what_ai_changes_in_schools\",\"evidence_based_revision\",\"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/03-01-what-ai-changes-in-schools-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"03.01\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, the **ask** move gives a concise explanation tied to **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)**, and to AI-TPACK as the purpose-fit anchor for generative tools specifically (Ning et al., 2024).\n- If your note shows **confusion**, the **explain** move compares a weak and strong version of the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision.\n- If your note is an **insight**, the **represent** move transfers the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, the **practice** move shows where the connection changes the artifact.\n\nThese four support moves (ask, explain, represent, practice) are the same actions the misconception rail routes to, so the displayed move and the routed support always use identical terms.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision for CAT 531 03.01. A peer comment points to a evidence move issue connected to critical edtech studies. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which trace would make the revision easiest to review?\" options='[\"A before-after artifact record linked to the decision you made and the evidence you used.\", \"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"0\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_what_ai_changes_in_schools\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision captures instructional purpose, equity or privacy boundary, support request, revision rationale, and confidence change.\n\n## References\n\n- Mishra, P., & Koehler, M. J. (2006). Technological pedagogical content knowledge: A framework for teacher knowledge. Teachers College Record, 108(6), 1017-1054. https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- Ning, Y., et al. (2024). Teachers' AI-TPACK: Exploring the relationship between knowledge elements. Sustainability, 16(3), 978. https://doi.org/10.3390/su16030978\n- Selwyn, N. (2016). Distrusting Educational Technology: Critical Questions for Changing Times. Routledge. https://doi.org/10.4324/9781315670160\n- Perrotta, C., & Selwyn, N. (2019). Deep learning goes to school: Toward a relational understanding of AI in education. Learning, Media and Technology, 45(3), 251-269. https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017\n- Mecoli, S. (2013). The influence of the pedagogical content knowledge theoretical framework on research on preservice teacher education. Journal of Education, 193(3), 21-28. https://doi.org/10.1177/002205741319300304\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_3_1_what_ai_changes_in_schools_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_what_ai_changes_in_schools","stem":"A teacher reports that an AI feedback tool cut her essay-grading time from six hours to ninety minutes and wants to adopt it. Which statement best justifies the decision as a defensible technology choice?","options":["The ninety-minute time saving alone shows the tool improves the class.","The tool returns comments fast, so students will read them more often.","The saved time is reinvested in conferencing with the four students whose drafts the tool flagged as off-topic, a learning activity she could not run before.","The tool is the newest product the district licensed this year."],"correct_index":2,"explanation":"A defensible choice names the learning activity the change enables, not just the labor saved. Time saved is only justification when it is connected to a specific instructional gain.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_3_1_efficiency_equals_better_teaching","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_ai_in_education","cat531_supplement_what_ai_changes_in_schools","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_3_1_what_ai_changes_in_schools_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_what_ai_changes_in_schools","stem":"A colleague says, \"This reading-recommendation app is just a tool, so any equity problems come from how teachers use it, not the app itself.\" What is the strongest reply?","options":["Agree, because software cannot be biased on its own.","The app's recommendation model was trained on past borrowing data, so it can steer striving readers toward easier books regardless of teacher intent.","Agree, because the teacher chooses which students log in.","The app is fine because students enjoy using it."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Tools embed design and data assumptions that shape outcomes independent of teacher use. A recommender trained on past borrowing data inherits and amplifies those patterns; the recommender-systems fairness literature documents exactly this feedback-loop bias, where yesterday's popular choices steer today's exposure (Wang et al., 2022, ACM TOIS survey on recommender fairness). Naming the built-in assumption is the AI-literacy move.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_3_1_tool_neutrality","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_ai_in_education","cat531_supplement_what_ai_changes_in_schools","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_3_1_what_ai_changes_in_schools_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_what_ai_changes_in_schools","stem":"A teacher plans to let an AI chatbot generate and answer the comprehension questions for a novel unit so students can move faster. Which concern most directly belongs in her decision?","options":["Whether the chatbot's interface matches the school color scheme.","Whether students enjoy chatting with the bot.","Whether generating and answering the questions removes the close-reading and inference practice that was the point of the unit.","Whether the bot can produce more questions than the teacher could."],"correct_index":2,"explanation":"When AI takes over a task, name the student thinking it displaces. If the displaced work was the learning target, automating it defeats the lesson.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_3_1_displacement_blindness","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_ai_in_education","cat531_supplement_what_ai_changes_in_schools","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_3_1_what_ai_changes_in_schools_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_what_ai_changes_in_schools","stem":"Two third-grade teachers must choose ONE tool for a fluency block: Tool A is a flashy new AI tutor with animated rewards and no stated learning objective, Tool B is a plain dashboard that flags which phonics patterns each student misses. Which decision and reason is most defensible?","options":["Tool A, because its novelty will raise engagement on its own.","Tool B, because the flagged phonics patterns connect directly to the fluency objective and let the teacher target small-group instruction.","Tool A, because students will ask to use it again.","Either tool, since both are AI and the choice does not matter."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The instructional purpose, here targeting specific phonics gaps, drives the choice. Novelty and engagement are not substitutes for a named learning purpose.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_3_1_novelty_as_purpose","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_ai_in_education","cat531_supplement_what_ai_changes_in_schools","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_3_1_efficiency_equals_better_teaching","pattern":"speed_mistaken_for_learning_gain","description":"Assuming that because an AI tool makes a teaching task faster it has improved student learning","trigger":"teacher justifies adopting a tool only by time saved on grading, planning, or feedback","feedback":"Separate the labor change from the learning change. Name the specific learning activity the tool improves and the evidence you would inspect, not just the minutes saved.","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_3_1_tool_neutrality","pattern":"ai_tool_treated_as_neutral","description":"Believing an AI tool is a neutral instrument whose effects depend only on how the teacher uses it","trigger":"teacher dismisses equity or data concerns by saying the tool is just a tool","feedback":"A tool carries design assumptions about who it serves and what data it collects. Name one assumption built into this tool and how it shifts who benefits in your classroom.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_3_1_novelty_as_purpose","pattern":"novelty_substituted_for_instructional_purpose","description":"Choosing an AI tool because it is new or engaging rather than because it serves a named instructional purpose","trigger":"teacher cannot state the learning goal the tool serves but cites student excitement","feedback":"Write the instructional purpose first, then ask whether this tool serves it better than your current approach. Excitement is not the purpose.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_3_1_displacement_blindness","pattern":"ignoring_what_the_tool_displaces","description":"Adopting AI for a task without noticing what valuable practice or judgment it removes from students","trigger":"teacher adds an AI step without naming the student thinking it replaces","feedback":"For every task AI takes over, name the cognitive work students no longer do, then decide whether that work was the point of the lesson.","rail_action":"practice"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"c3ceb244408814ed6d5b0310e57f7dfd6374e08c291508de9de87062c5d86737","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}